%\begin{comment}
Pruning and quantization are widely used to compress neural networks, yet the
role of entropy in their compressed representations remains comparatively
underexplored. We introduce METaQ, a quantization-aware training (QAT) method
that jointly optimizes predictive accuracy and the compressibility of the
weight representation.

METaQ employs learned-step uniform quantization and introduces an inner problem
in perspective form. The mass assigned to the zero symbol controls sparsity,
whereas relaxed counts of the remaining symbols induce an entropy-based term.
Envelope derivatives of the inner problem provide gradient contributions for
both the latent weights and the quantization step sizes. METaQ therefore learns
the network weights, the locations of the quantization levels, and the symbol
distribution used by the downstream lossless encoder within a unified training
objective. \textcolor{red}{[...]}
%\end{comment}
\end{abstract}

