\end{table}

\FloatBarrier

For AlexNet, the LSQ-only control ($T_2=0$, test~213) retained a quantized
top-1 accuracy of $56.16\%$ and produced approximately $15\%$ natural
sparsity through the LSQ zero bin. Activating the METaQ zero-mass rule
$z_i>0.5$ with $(T_1,T_2,T_3)=(10^{-5},7.7\times10^{-9},10^{-7})$
(test~216) yielded a stable $80.01\%$ sparsity and a sparse-package ratio of
$3.25\%$, corresponding to a $30.77\times$ reduction relative to FP32.
The delivered sparse accuracy was $53.99\%$, i.e., $2.50$ percentage points
below the $56.48\%$ FP32 baseline. These runs validate $T_2$ as a quantitative
sparsity control, but are an ablation rather than a final lossless operating
point.

The implementation distinguishes two sources of zeros. LSQ creates a zero
symbol whenever the latent weight falls in the interval mapped to the LSQ
zero code. METaQ can additionally apply the hard deployment rule
$z_i>0.5$, where $z_i=1-y_i$ is the relaxed mass assigned to the zero symbol.
The mask used by this rule is shared by the training forward pass, evaluation,
and serialization. The experiments therefore isolate the sparsity mechanism
implemented by the relaxed zero-symbol mass and its deployment threshold.

\subsection{Controlled Baseline Comparison}

The final comparison will rerun each baseline from the same pretrained
checkpoint, using the same quantized tensors, codebook size, validation set,
and byte-level accounting. This controlled protocol is necessary because a
nominal bit width does not include entropy-coding gains or metadata overhead.
Table~\ref{tab:controlled-comparison} is reserved for the ResNet--18 comparison;
the discussion of statistical significance and state-of-the-art positioning
